'''Ayorin''', officially the '''Federation of Ayorin''' ({{langx|yo|Igbimọ ti Ayọ̀rìn}}; {{langx|ig|Njikọ Áyóra}}) is a [[W:Sovereign state|sovereign state]] in [[South Kaftia]]. It shares land borders with [[Dakare]] to the southwest, [[Tamazgha]] to the north, [[Enfolo]] to the east, and a maritime border with [[Sanyan]]. The federal capital of Ayorin is [[Ibadan]] ({{Literal translation|between forests}}), while its largest city is [[Aarin|Ààrín]] ({{Literal translation|middle settlement}}). With an area of {{Convert|595,485.15|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}, Ayorin is the #-largest country in Southern Kaftia, the # largest country on the Kaftian continent, and the # largest country in the the world. With an estimated population of approximately 90 million in 2026, Ayorin is the # most populous on Kaftia and the # most populous on [[Adonia]].

Ayorin's constitution defines the country as a [[W:Federalism|federal]] [[W:Parliamentary system|parliamentary]] [[W:Republic|republic]]. The southern half of the country is predominantly adherent to traditional Ayori religion (known as [[Ìbẹmìílò]] and its various branches, like [[Ẹnimimọ]]), while the northern half has a plurality of followers of [[W:Christianity|Christianity]] (predominately the [[Zamarra Catholic Church]]) that is heavily [[W:Syncretism|syncretized]] with Ìbẹmìílò beliefs and practices. With an area of {{Convert|595,485.15|km2|sqmi|abbr=on}}, Ayorin is the #-largest country in Southern Kaftia, the # largest country on the Kaftian continent, and the # largest country in the the world. With a population of approximately 90 million in 2026, Ayorin is the # most populous on Kaftia and the # most populous on Adonia.

The first state to unify the Ayori city-states emerged in 1248 following the conquests of [[Tiwalade II]], who declared himself Tiwalade I of the [[Owuro Empire]]. Throughout the 17th and 18th centuries, Owuro faced pressure from [[Buurawaat]]<nowiki/>and the [[Breislandic Empire]], including forced concessions of territory; nevertheless, it successfully resisted forced subjugation to a foreign power. By 1789 Breisland had gained all Owuro land north of the XX Parallel (later becoming know as the [[Line of Servitude]]), while all territories south of the line became a protectorate of the [[Empire of Burawa]] in the [[Treaty of Wari]], and was reorganized as the autonomous [[Ayori Free State]]. Following the end of the [[Great Adonian War]], anti-colonial sentiment led to the unification of the newly-independent [[North Ayorin]] and [[South Ayorin]], establishing the modern [[Borders of Ayorin|Ayori borders]]. Unresolved [[Internal conflict in Ayorin|political, linguistic and religious tensions]] sparked a [[Ayori Civil War|destructive civil war]] in the country. [[International intervention in the Ayori Civil War|International intervention]] brought about the end of the civil war, while the 1960 [[Noé Agreement]] established Ayorin's current federal political system.

Ayorin has Kaftia's fourth largest economy. It is the fastest growing economy in Kaftia and is considered one of the great emerging powers in the region. Ayorin was one of the founding members of the [[Kaftian Union]] and is member of most major international organizations. The economy has historically been fueled by extraction of natural resources, but is expanding in the sectors of science, tourism, commerce and [[W:Medical tourism|medical tourism]]. Ayorin is recognized as a [[W:Newly industrialized country|newly industrialized country]], and is ranked as the ?? largest in the world.

== Etymology ==
{{Main|Name of Ayorin}}
The name ''Ayorin'' derives from the [[Imo language|Imo]] compound word ''Ayọ̀rìn'', composed of two roots: ''Ayọ̀'' ({{Literal translation|joy|happiness|delight}}) and ''Rìn'' ({{Literal translation|walk|journey|travel}}).

The literal meaning of ''Ayọ̀rìn'' is thus "joy walks" or "one who journeys/travels in joy." The name was first applied to the region during the formation of the [[Owuro Empire]] in the 13th century, when [[Tiwalade I]]—then known as Tiwalade II of the city-state of [[Oyo (city-state)|Oyo]]—unified the [[Ayori city-states]] under a single banner. According to oral tradition, Tiwalade chose the name to symbolize the territory's long history as an important [[W:Stopover|place of rest]] for traders making the dangeous journey between [[North Kaftia|North]] and [[South Kaftia]], and also representing his vision of a prosperous realm where his people could "walk in joy" free from the constant warfare between rival city-states.

In the [[Ekwe language|Ekwu language]], the country is called {{lang|ig|Ala Ayorin}} ("Land of Ayorin") or, more traditionally, ''Ala Oma'' ({{lang|ig|Ala Ọma}}, "Good Land" or "Beautiful Land"). The Ekwu name ''Ala Oma'' predates the federation and was used by Ekwu-speaking peoples to refer to the fertile southern territories that would later be conquered by the Owuro Empire. Following the [[Treaty of Wari]] (1789) and the establishment of the [[Ayori Free State]], the name ''Ayorin'' was officially adopted as the designation for the protectorate, replacing earlier colonial designations.

=== Historical usage ===
Prior to the 13th century, the term ''Ayorin'' was not used as a geographic or political designation. The region was known by various names among its diverse peoples:

* The Imo-speaking city-states collectively referred to their lands as ''Ilẹ̀ Ayọ̀'' ("Land of Joy") or simply by individual city names.
* The Ekwu-speaking territories were known as ''Ala Ekwe'' ("Ekwe Land") or by clan and village names.
* Northern traders from [[Tamazgha]] and southern trades from the [[Empire of Burawa]] referred to the region as the "Land of Rivers" or the "River Kingdoms."

The unification under Tiwalade I marked the first use of ''Ayorin'' as a supra-ethnic designation intended to encompass all subjects of the Owuro Empire, regardless of linguistic or cultural background. This inclusive naming convention was unusual for its time and is credited with helping forge a shared Ayori identity that persisted through centuries of foreign pressure and colonial rule.

