=== Independence and modernization ===
{{Main|Republic of Ayorin|Àtúndá}}

Following South Ayorin's independence from Burawa, Aarin became the center of the resurgence of Ayori cultural identity. Rapid political changes in Burawa brought about [[Policy 1442]] and the subsequent [[breakup of Burawa]]. The Republic of Ayorin along with four other states declared independence from Burawa on 10 March 1926. Very quickly, the Burawa-backed [[Kadarist Party]] under [[Chijindum Newke]] came to dominate Ayori politics. His government adopted [[W:Nationalization|nationalization]] and encouraged industrialization and [[Ayorin-Burawa relations|pro-Burawa]] and anti-Breislandic policies. The years following the conclusion of Ayorin's separation from Burawa involved intense nationalization and economic reforms.
[[File:W.E.B. Du Bois by James E. Purdy, 1907 (cropped).jpg|left|thumb|358x358px|[[Olamilekan Nnamani]] arose during the [[Àtúndá|Ayori Renaissance]] as one of the most influential philosophers of Kaftia.]]
Domestically, Newke led the nation through a period of rapid modernization, introducing reforms such as [[W:Free education|free education]] through university, and [[W:Universal healthcare|universal healthcare]] for all residents of Ayorin, a relatively peaceful redistribution of land. The availability of medicine and access to clinics was vastly expanded, into the hopes of suppressing and ultimately eradicating tropical diseases. He oversaw the [[Women's rights in Ayorin|elevation of women]] to positions of power, while expanding the rights of minorities and [[Christianity in Ayorin|Christians]] alike. Over three million people would eventually be lifted out of poverty during the duration of the Republic, establishing highly visible middle-class communities throughout Ayorin. New roads, bridges, railways, hospitals and schools spread throughout the country with funds from the member states of the [[Socialist Bloc]].

Most of the New Ayori cultural movement arose from a generation that had grown up as citizens of Burawa, and thus had access to popular [[Mesogean philosophy]] and cultural works at the time. A majority of them benefited by paternal investment in cultural capital, including better-than-average education. Many in this new movement were students, writers, and artists returning from long-established higher education institutions in Burawa. Armed with their education, this new generation of Ayori sought a better [[W:Standard of living|standard of living]] and to create their own unique cultural products. At its heart, this movement embodied the absorption of foreign ideas and technology (collectively referred to as ''"ajeji"'', meaning "alien") and fusing them with Ayori cultural history to form new, unique cultural expressions.

The rise in ethnic consciousness, known as the ''[[Àtúndá]]'' ({{Literal translation|renewal|revival}}), and known internationally as the "Ayori Renaissance" was successful in that it brought renewed focus on the neglected status of traditional Ayori cultural practices which had been suppressed under colonial pressure throughout the preceding century. Not only through an expansion of culture, but on a sociological level, did the Renaissance reshape how Ayori (both in the country and in the [[Ayori diaspora]]), viewed their heritage. The migration of rural Ayori to the industrial centers of [[Aarin]] and [[Naji]] provided for a large shift in Ayori society, one from more close-knit tribal groups, to one of urban development marked by cosmopolitan sophistication. This new identity led to a greater social consciousness, and Ayori people became a cultural powerhouse in Kaftia, expanding intellectual and social contacts internationally.

The progress—both symbolic and real—during this period became a point of reference from which the Ayori government sought to cultivate a renewed sense of [[W:Self-determination|self-determination]] and unity that provided a growing sense of both urbanity and empowerment, as well as an example for the colonized Kaftian peoples to build upon for the [[Decolonization of Kaftia|Kaftian struggle for decolonization]] in the 1950s and 1960s. The expanding urban setting of the emerging urban centers provided a venue for Ayori of all ethnic and linguistic backgrounds to experience a variety of lifestyles and culture that was previously reserved for on the most privileged under colonial rule. Through this expression, the Renaissance encouraged renewed appreciation and practicing of traditional Ayori culture. For instance, once-suppressed oral traditions (like the [[W:griot|alágbèsè]]) and [[Ìbẹmìílò]] teachings provided a rich source for the emerging artistic and intellectual imagination, which provided a long sought connection to traditional Ayori culture.

==== Reunification ====
{{Main|Reunification of Ayorin}}

Following the outbreak of the [[Great Adonian War]], Ayorin was heavily inclined to support the Socialist Bloc, in the hopes that it would bring about the liberation of the Northern states from colonial occupation. This led to increasingly aggressive postering by the Ayori government, which supported infiltrators and guerrillas in North Ayorin in order to harass and weaken the Breislandic colonial administration. Border skirmishes would occasionally occur across the Line of Servitude and on the outskirts of Aarin. This triggered a mobilization by the colonial government, which raised significant forces to mitigate the threat of an Ayori invasion. The situation persisted until Burawa threw it's support behind Breisland and the Allies following the [[Volisanian invasion of Burawa]], resulting in the end of Burawi support for Ayori aspirations for forceful reunification.

President NAME of North Ayorin (left) and President NAME of South Ayorin (right) jointly announcing reunification on 18 May 1951 in Aarin

After the end of the Great Adonian War, the [[XY memorandum]] instituted [[W:Majority rule|majority rule]] in [[Breislandic Ayorin]] full autonomy in its powers (1950). The ruling [[Freedom and Unity Party]] (FUP), which was still propped up by Breisland would entered negotiations with its southern neighbor, the South Ayorin on achieving the reunification of the two states. The dominant [[Socialist Party of Ayorin|Socialist Party of South Ayorin]] accepted, beginning negotiations in 1951 on a merger of the two states. Despite the Southern government's enthusiasm, it was not shared by large swathes of the population, believing that the gap between the two states were too wide to bridge. Having been under an extractive colonial regime for over a century, North Ayorin was relatively [[W:Least developed countries|underdeveloped]], and a significant portion (upwards of 40%) of the population spoke [[W:German language|Breislandic]], which was almost universally absent in the South.

On 18 May 1951, the two governments signed the [[Articles of Unification]], officially merging the states into the [[Democratic Republic of Ayorin]]. The two governments agreed on establishing their administration in [[Aarin]], which was symbolic of the reunification due to its location on the [[Line of Servitude]] which had previously split the city in half. For the first two years after reunification, a [[W:Provisional government|provisional government]] were to be shared by the two parties, with half percent of all secretary positions and half of all parliamentary seats going to the Socialists, and vice versa for the FUP, with the agreement that general elections would be held in 1954 at the latest.

